      "knowledge_point": "Using the Double-Angle Formula Multiple Times",
      "problem": "$4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{csc}⁡({140}^{∘})=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={70}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({70}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot {70}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({140}^{∘})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{csc}⁡({140}^{∘}) & =2\\cdot [2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({70}^{∘})]\\operatorname{csc}⁡({140}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({140}^{∘})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({140}^{∘})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
